{
  "title": "What time is it?",
  "title_target": "Saat kaç?",
  "goal": "Tell the time, the day and the month; say when something happens; and put together your daily schedule.",
  "bekal": [
    "answer *saat kaç* and ask *saat kaçta*",
    "name the seven days and the twelve months",
    "use frequency words: *her gün*, *bazen*, *hiç*"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Dialogue — Ders programı",
      "intro": "First day of the course. In the teachers' room, Ayşe goes over the schedule with Budi.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Ayşe:",
          "text": "Budi, dersler pazartesi başlıyor.",
          "id": "Budi, classes start on Monday.",
          "literal": "Budi, lesson-s Monday is-starting."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Saat kaçta?",
          "id": "At what time?",
          "literal": "hour how-many-at?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ayşe:",
          "text": "Sabah dokuzda. Öğlene kadar devam ediyor.",
          "id": "At nine in the morning. They go on until noon.",
          "literal": "morning nine-at. noon-to until continuation is-doing."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Her gün mü?",
          "id": "Every day?",
          "literal": "every day question?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ayşe:",
          "text": "Hayır. Pazartesi, çarşamba ve cuma. Haftada üç gün.",
          "id": "No. Monday, Wednesday and Friday. Three days a week.",
          "literal": "no. Monday, Wednesday and Friday. week-in three day."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Peki salı ve perşembe ne yapıyorum?",
          "id": "So what do I do on Tuesday and Thursday?",
          "literal": "well Tuesday and Thursday what am-doing-I?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ayşe:",
          "text": "Evde çalışıyorsun! Ödev veriyorum.",
          "id": "You study at home! I give homework.",
          "literal": "home-at are-studying-you! homework am-giving-I."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Anladım. Hafta sonu ders var mı?",
          "id": "Got it. Are there classes on the weekend?",
          "literal": "understood-I. week end-its lesson there-is question?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ayşe:",
          "text": "Cumartesi bazen var, pazar hiç yok.",
          "id": "Sometimes on Saturday, never on Sunday.",
          "literal": "Saturday sometimes there-is, Sunday at-all there-isn't."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Sınav ne zaman?",
          "id": "When's the exam?",
          "literal": "exam what time?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ayşe:",
          "text": "Aralık ayında, ayın on beşinde.",
          "id": "In December, on the fifteenth.",
          "literal": "December month-its-in, month-'s ten five-its-on."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Çok vaktim var o zaman. Teşekkürler!",
          "id": "Then I've got plenty of time. Thanks!",
          "literal": "much time-my there-is that time. thanks-plural!"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**Saat kaç? asks the time; saat kaçta? asks when.** The first wants a number (\"Saat üç\" — it's three o'clock). The second wants an appointment (\"Saat üçte\" — at three o'clock). One ending apart, two different questions.",
        "**Day names aren't capitalized when they stand alone in mid-sentence** — but they are when they refer to a specific date. In everyday practice Turks capitalize both, and nobody minds.",
        "**Haftada üç gün** — \"three days in a week\". To express frequency, Turkish puts *-de* on the unit of time: *günde iki kere* (twice a day), *ayda bir* (once a month), *yılda dört kez* (four times a year).",
        "**Hiç means \"not at all\" or \"ever\".** With a negative sentence it adds force: *hiç yok* (there's none at all), *hiç bilmiyorum* (I have no idea at all). In a question it means \"ever\": *Hiç İstanbul'a gittin mi?* — \"Have you ever been to Istanbul?\""
      ]
    }
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Telling the time",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "For the full hour, just *saat* + number. For half past, add *buçuk*. For quarters there are two phrases: *çeyrek geçiyor* (quarter past) and *çeyrek var* (quarter to)."
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Time",
            "Türkçe",
            "Say it"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "3:00",
              "Saat üç.",
              "sa-AT üch"
            ],
            [
              "3:30",
              "Saat üç buçuk.",
              "sa-AT üch bu-CHUK"
            ],
            [
              "3:15",
              "Üçü çeyrek geçiyor.",
              "ü-CHÜ chey-REK ge-CHI-yor"
            ],
            [
              "3:45",
              "Dörde çeyrek var.",
              "dör-DE chey-REK var"
            ],
            [
              "12:00",
              "Saat on iki.",
              "sa-AT on i-KI"
            ],
            [
              "12:30",
              "Yarım.",
              "ya-RUHM"
            ],
            [
              "9:00 a.m.",
              "Sabah dokuz.",
              "sa-BAH do-KUZ"
            ],
            [
              "9:00 p.m.",
              "Akşam dokuz.",
              "ak-SHAM do-KUZ"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Half past twelve has a word of its own: **yarım** (12:30), and noon is *öğlen*. Don't confuse *yarım* with *buçuk*, which always attaches to another number."
        },
        {
          "type": "sub",
          "text": "At what time — the ending -de"
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Türkçe",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Saat kaçta?",
              "At what time?"
            ],
            [
              "Saat üçte.",
              "At three."
            ],
            [
              "Saat dokuzda.",
              "At nine."
            ],
            [
              "Saat yedide.",
              "At seven."
            ],
            [
              "Üç buçukta.",
              "At half past three."
            ],
            [
              "Sabah erken.",
              "Early in the morning."
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "kotak",
          "title": "Tricky numbers",
          "items": [
            "*Bir* takes *-de*: *birde*; *altı* and *yedi* become *altıda*, *yedide*. Numbers ending in a hard consonant take *-te*: *beşte*, *üçte*, *dörtte* — note the two t's in *dörtte*.",
            "The safest method: say the number, listen to its last letter, then apply Fıstıkçı Şahap from Unit 5."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
