        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Time words go **after the subject and before the verb**: *Ben her gün Türkçe çalışıyorum*. This order is flexible — the only thing that can't change is the verb's place at the end."
        },
        {
          "type": "kotak",
          "title": "Ay means two things",
          "items": [
            "*Ay* is both the moon in the sky and the month on the calendar — just as English *moon* and *month* come from the same old root. The Turkish flag has an *ay yıldız*, \"moon and star\".",
            "*Ay!* is also a cry of surprise or pain, like \"ow!\". Context tells them apart, and they never get mixed up."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "kotak",
          "title": "Unit 5 review",
          "items": [
            "Cover the Turkish and rebuild it from memory: \"Where are you?\" · \"I'm at school.\" · \"There's a bank across from the mosque.\" · \"The book is on the table.\" · \"No problem.\"",
            "Then add the right location ending to: *kitap*, *sınıf*, *Ankara*, *ev*."
          ]
        }
